-- ============================================================================
-- Migration 78: entity_alias — declare the four fields its writer already writes
-- (Track PC.2), and repair the rows that predate them.
--
-- THE DRIFT. `entity_alias` is SCHEMAFULL and migration 39 declares five fields:
-- alias_text, canonical_entity, source_document, confidence, created_at.
-- `EntityRepository.register_alias` sets `match_type`, `similarity_score`,
-- `method` and `verified` on top, and SurrealDB drops undeclared fields on a
-- SCHEMAFULL table SILENTLY rather than raising. So on any freshly migrated
-- database every alias loses its provenance — how it was matched, how strongly,
-- by which method, and whether a human confirmed it — with no error and no log.
-- `vault_sync_service` exports only `verified = true` aliases, so on such a
-- database it can never export a single one.
--
-- Found by PC.2's `find_by_alias` ordering fix, which sorts by
-- `verified DESC, similarity_score DESC, id ASC` so one surface form binds to one
-- canonical reproducibly. Its test passes against the long-lived `staging`
-- database, where all nine columns exist because the table was written before it
-- was schema-locked, and FAILS against a fresh container, where all three sort
-- keys read NONE.
--
-- THE SECOND HALF — why the DEFINE alone is not enough. A SurrealDB column
-- DEFAULT applies only to NEWLY created records; rows that predate the DEFINE
-- keep NONE, and a strict (non-`option<>`) type then rejects the whole record on
-- the first UPDATE, because a SCHEMAFULL UPDATE re-validates every field. This
-- repository has hit that exact class twice — migration 61 (`entity`) and
-- migration 64 (`source`) — and migration 65 turned the repair into an idempotent
-- forward-guard sweep. 65 runs BEFORE this migration, so it cannot know about a
-- field defined here; the coalesce therefore belongs in 78 itself.
--
-- `entity_alias` has live UPDATE paths that would break on a drifted row:
--   * entity_filtering/deduplication/canonical_entities.py — the K.2 duplicate
--     merge transfers aliases with `UPDATE entity_alias SET canonical_entity = ...`
--   * services/extraction/api.py — the same transfer
--   * recanonicalization_service.py — the K.3 apply
--   * vault_sync_service.py — the vault round trip, which sets all four fields
--
-- WHY `verified` IS STRICT AND THE OTHER THREE ARE NOT. `verified` carries a
-- policy (`find_by_alias` ranks by it; the vault export filters on it), and a
-- tri-state boolean makes both meaningless — NONE sorts by engine-defined order
-- and satisfies neither `= true` nor `= false`. The other three are genuinely
-- absent for aliases created outside the resolver, so `option<>` is the honest
-- type there and no coalesce is needed or wanted.
--
-- The UPDATE is idempotent: on a clean row `verified ?? false` is a no-op; on a
-- drifted row it sets only the NONE field. Unverified is the safe direction —
-- ranked below a verified alias and excluded from the vault export.
-- ============================================================================

DEFINE FIELD IF NOT EXISTS match_type ON entity_alias TYPE option<string>;
DEFINE FIELD IF NOT EXISTS similarity_score ON entity_alias TYPE option<float>;
DEFINE FIELD IF NOT EXISTS method ON entity_alias TYPE option<string>;
DEFINE FIELD IF NOT EXISTS verified ON entity_alias TYPE bool DEFAULT false;

UPDATE entity_alias SET verified = verified ?? false;
